From: Chris Trowbridge <chris.trowbridge@ezurio.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] openssl: load config into private library context

When the OPENSSL_CONF environment variable is set, load its configuration
into CST's private OSSL_LIB_CTX. This allows external OpenSSL providers
(such as pkcs11-provider for AWS KMS signing) to be activated without
modifying CST's own code.

The load is non-fatal: if the configuration file does not exist or does
not contain provider sections, CST continues with only the default
provider (i.e. normal filesystem-based key loading).

This enables transparent PKCS#11/HSM-based signing when PEM wrapper files
(containing PKCS#11 URIs) are placed at the expected private key paths.
Without OPENSSL_CONF set, behavior is unchanged.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [OE-specific]

Signed-off-by: Chris Trowbridge <chris.trowbridge@ezurio.com>
---
 cst-4.0.1/src/lib/common/openssl_helper.c | 12 ++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+)

diff --git a/cst-4.0.1/src/lib/common/openssl_helper.c b/cst-4.0.1/src/lib/common/openssl_helper.c
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/cst-4.0.1/src/lib/common/openssl_helper.c
+++ b/cst-4.0.1/src/lib/common/openssl_helper.c
@@ -192,5 +192,17 @@ void openssl_initialize(void)
         error("Error loading default provider");
     }
+
+    /* If OPENSSL_CONF is set, load its provider configuration into the
+     * private library context. This enables provider-based key loading
+     * (e.g. pkcs11-provider for HSM/KMS signing) without requiring
+     * changes to CST's signing logic. Non-fatal if config has no
+     * provider sections or the file is absent. */
+    const char *openssl_conf = getenv("OPENSSL_CONF");
+    if (openssl_conf) {
+        OSSL_LIB_CTX_load_config(g_libctx, openssl_conf);
+        ERR_clear_error();
+    }
+
 #if CST_WITH_PQC
     /* Load OQS provider */
     if (OSSL_PROVIDER_add_builtin(g_libctx, "oqsprovider", oqs_provider_init) !=
